[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its own reports show three problems.
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: `parser` fixed, `hint` won't fix because the booking-v3
form is retired. Neither change touches the third problem.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but the one-day move is still open. Neither change fixes it.
</Callout>

## The problems

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table counts the one-day moves under `parser` and footnotes them. They are a problem of their
own, so they have their own ticket and leave the `parser` ticket. `hint` has a ticket of its own
now too.

One ticket per problem:

- `parser`: [Fajkowsky/support-kb#1](https://github.com/Fajkowsky/support-kb/issues/1), fixed
- `hint`: [Fajkowsky/support-kb#2](https://github.com/Fajkowsky/support-kb/issues/2), won't fix
- `one-day move`: [Fajkowsky/support-kb#3](https://github.com/Fajkowsky/support-kb/issues/3), **open**

### `parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The parser reads `02/12` as 12 February when the
tenant meant 2 December. In every report that quotes two dates, both day numbers are 12 or
lower, so the misread parses cleanly and nothing raises an error. Fixed in
`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. If a day-first tenant still reports a flip, check that
the fix asks the locale, as `causes.ts` does, rather than a list of EU locales: `en-GB` writes the
day first too.

### `hint`: booking-v3's form tells people to type the month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. A day-first tenant's people type what the
hint says, and the form reads the date the tenant's way. The symptom matches the parser bug, but
the parser fix does not touch this code path. Only booking-v3 tenants, and only reports that
came in through the form. Won't fix: the booking-v3 form is retired.

### `one-day move`: the date shifts by a day, day and month stay put

These reports describe a different failure. Tuesday shows as Monday. A client in Lisbon gets
Tuesday while a client in Toronto gets Monday for the same meeting. An invite arrives before its
booking. June bookings are one day off. The desk's notes already flag them, but the cause table
on #4471 files them under `parser` because it looks up the tenant only. A time-zone or
daylight-saving conversion at a date boundary fits these reports. That is a lead, not a finding.
Still open: the parser fix reads day and month in the right order, and a shifted day has both
right.

## How the reports were counted

- One report per id. The ticket system exports overlapping pages, so some reports appear twice.
- Cause by tenant: locale and booking form from the tenant directory, through `causes.ts`.
  Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. It disagrees with the tenant often, so it
  decides nothing here.
- One-day moves from `desk-notes.json`: the table footnotes them under `parser`; their ticket
  counts them on their own.

## Lessons

- Merge on cause, not on symptom. All three problems produce the same symptom, a wrong date, so
  every subject line looked alike. A new decision record should replace
  [0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject); 0001 stays as written.
- Read what the page shows. The cause table on #4471 already showed a second cause while the
  words above it said one issue.

